<section class="reference-section" id="vamana-king-bali"><div class="reference-section-heading"><h2>Vamana and King Bali · The Three Steps</h2><a href="/vamana-king-bali.html">Return to story</a></div><div class="reference-content"><p><strong>Srimad Bhagavat Mahapuran · Gita Press Sanskrit–Hindi edition, first volume.</strong> Original English retelling based only on Skandha 8, chapters 15–23 of the owner-supplied edition. The book scan is not distributed by this website.</p><div class="source-list"><a href="https://archive.org/details/srimad-bhagavat-mahapuran-2-volume-set-sanskrit-hindi/Srimad%20Bhagavat%20Mahapuran%20Volume%201%20Sanskrit%20Hindi/mode/2up" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><span>Open the supplied first volume</span><small>Internet Archive · Gita Press Sanskrit–Hindi edition</small></a></div><ol><li><strong>8.15</strong><small>Bali’s restoration through the Bhrigus, the Vishvajit sacrifice and his conquest of heaven.</small></li><li><strong>8.16</strong><small>Kashyapa instructs Aditi in the payo-vrata.</small></li><li><strong>8.17</strong><small>Vishnu appears to Aditi, grants her request and enters as her son.</small></li><li><strong>8.18.1–32</strong><small>Vamana’s appearance, brahmachari investiture, journey to Bali’s sacrifice and welcome.</small></li><li><strong>8.19.1–43</strong><small>The request for three steps, the teaching on contentment and Shukracharya’s warning.</small></li><li><strong>8.20.1–34</strong><small>Bali keeps his word; Vindhyavali brings the vessel; Vamana expands as Trivikrama and takes two steps.</small></li><li><strong>8.21.1–34</strong><small>The cosmic foot is honoured, Bali restrains his followers, is bound and offers his head for the third step.</small></li><li><strong>8.22.1–36</strong><small>Bali remains steadfast; Prahlada, Vindhyavali and Brahma speak; Vishnu grants Sutala and protection.</small></li><li><strong>8.23.1–31</strong><small>Bali is released and enters Sutala; Prahlada follows; Indra is restored to heaven.</small></li></ol><p>The retelling preserves the supplied edition’s sequence from Bali’s conquest through the gift of Sutala. Ritual directions, extended praise and combat are condensed. Later regional and festival traditions are not merged into this account.</p><p>Two reader paintings and one collection-card image are AI-generated interpretations created for single placements. Vamana’s brahmachari objects and Trivikrama’s Vishnu attributes follow devotional conventions; the cosmic form is interpretive rather than a literal inventory of every feature described in chapter 20. “A promise larger than possession” is an editorial reflection.</p></div></section>
